# Build order (ADR-021 waves)

You are working in **RayNu-V** (`vikkp/RayNu`). The product bar and the full
capability catalog live in [`docs/adr/ADR-021.md`](../../docs/adr/ADR-021.md).
Its **Build order** section is the order of work. The catalog letters (A–K)
are for reading, not for building. Work moves in **waves**; the tool knows
which wave is current.

## Ask the tool before choosing work

Trigger phrases: `what next`, `what's next`, `what should we build next`,
`what do I pick up`, `next gate`, `next task`, or any time you choose work
without the user naming it.

```bash
./tools/sync-sku-site.sh --next
```

It prints the current wave (the lowest numbered wave with a row not yet
`lived`), the rows to pick up in order with the **one iron thread** marked,
and host-first filler from the next wave for when the R640 is busy. Start
with the first row it prints unless the user names another. Do not pick a
row from a later wave for iron work.

## When a gate passes

An iron marker printed on the R640, or a host gate closed in CI. In the
**same change**:

1. Open `docs/adr/ADR-021.md`, find the row in **Catalog**, set **Status**
   to `lived`, and put the evidence in **Note** (commit SHA, iron marker,
   tool, or evidence doc). The sync tool rejects `lived` without evidence.
   Latitude / QEMU / nested do **not** make a row lived.
2. Run `./tools/sync-sku-site.sh` and `./tools/sync-sku-site.sh --check`.
   Stage **both** `docs/adr/ADR-021.md` and `site/sku.json`.
3. Run `./tools/sync-sku-site.sh --next`. If the wave closed, the current
   wave advanced; say so in the `docs/hda.md` changelog row for this commit
   (HDA procedure in `hda-update.mdc` still applies).
4. Begin the first row `--next` prints. If it is the iron thread and iron is
   not available, take the first host-first row instead and say why.

When code lands on a branch that is under iron test, set that row to
`in flight` and name the branch or PR in Note. When a row is demoted, record
it in the HDA changelog.

## Branching mantra

**Branch off main. Merge when the gate closes. Never chain.**

Start every task branch at `origin/main`, even if this run was opened on a
feature branch (fetch main, branch from it, say so in the PR). Merge to main
when the row's gate closes (host gate green in CI; iron row when the marker
printed and the catalog row is `lived`). Never base a branch on another open
feature branch; CI job `branching` fails a PR whose base is not `main`.
Open the PR against `main`.

## Rules

- **One iron thread at a time.** Each wave has exactly one row that needs
  the R640. Do not open a second iron thread while it is unclosed.
- **Host-only rows are filler, always available.** Never idle on the rack.
- **Final rows do not jump the queue.** A prospect asking is answered by the
  SKU page, not by pulling B13 / D14 / K rows forward.
- **Wave V (verification) runs continuously** beside every wave.
- **Wave 9 (72-hour iron soak) is not optional.** It is the Shipment 1 close.
- **Wave 0 is the lived base.** Every later wave re-runs it as regression.
- Adding a capability: add a catalog row **and** put its ID in exactly one
  wave row. The sync tool fails on a row in no wave or in two.
- Counts are not a score. HDA overall stays **99%**; do not derive a
  percent-to-GA from the catalog.

## Where it shows

- `site/sku.html` reads `site/sku.json` and shows **Now building: wave N**,
  the wave on every row, and the wave table.
- `docs/sku.md` is the card; `docs/m8_plan.md` M8.10+ is "the ADR-021 waves".
- Honesty constraints in `hda-update.mdc` and `iron-rollback.mdc` apply
  unchanged.
